  "result": "`summarize()` in `src/report.js` now handles 200,000 orders in well under a second. `npm test` passes, and the new tests check the output against the old implementation.\n\n**The cause:** it had three quadratic costs. Duplicate-id checks scanned an array, customer lookups used `groups.find`, and the whole leaderboard was re-sorted after every order.\n\n**The fix:**\n- Duplicate ids are tracked in a `Set`.\n- Customers are looked up through a `Map`.\n- The sort runs once at the end.\n\n**Why the output is unchanged:**\n- The ranking comparator never ties, because `customerId` is the final tie-break. Sorting once therefore gives the same order as sorting after each order.\n- Dedup still happens before the refund check, as before.\n- The rest of the logic is untouched.\n\n**Tests added in `test/report.test.js`:**\n- **Reference comparison:** the original implementation is kept as a reference and compared with `deepStrictEqual` on seeded random data. The data has many ties, duplicate ids, refunds, EUR amounts and several limits.\n- **Duplicate after refund:** a duplicate id is dropped even when its first copy was refunded.\n- **Performance:** 200,000 orders must finish in under 1 second. I didn't time it separately; it only checks the 1-second limit.\n\nOne edge case could differ: a `NaN` customerId. The old `===` lookup never matched it, so each such order made its own group, while a `Map` would merge them. Real customer ids are strings, so I left it alone.",
